{
  "image_name": "ffde8b87fe35bcca20798c27f949efad.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes strategies for separating chiral small molecules and their derivatized forms. At the top, a mixture of mostly ordinary molecules plus a pair of chiral molecules is shown in a sample tube. One route analyzes the small molecules directly, while another first adds CDRs to create derivatized molecules, then sends them to an achiral chromatography column. This suggests derivatization can convert enantiomers into species that can be resolved without a chiral column.\n\nThe direct-analysis route branches into three chiral separation methods. One is chiral capillary electrophoresis, where charged compounds move through a capillary and are distinguished by interactions in the separation medium. The other two are chromatographic approaches that rely either on a chiral stationary phase fixed to the column or on chiral reagents present in the mobile phase. In both cases, the chiral analytes interact differently from one another and from the nonchiral components, leading to separation.\n\nAll routes feed into a detector at the bottom, illustrated by distinct peaks over time. Overall, the diagram compares alternative workflows for resolving chiral molecules, emphasizing that enantiomer separation can be achieved either through inherently chiral separation environments or by derivatization followed by achiral chromatography.",
